  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C2.S2 — Robertson uncertainty relation for a qubit</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C2.S2 — Uncertainty</p></div><p>For the state \(|0\rangle\):</p><p>(a) Give \(\Delta X=\sqrt{\langle X^2\rangle-\langle X\rangle^2}\).</p><p>\(\Delta X=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Give the Robertson lower bound \(\tfrac12|\langle[X,Y]\rangle|\) for this state.</p><p>bound \(=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(X^2=I\) so \(\langle X^2\rangle=1\); and \(\langle 0|X|0\rangle=0\). Hence \(\Delta X=1\). By symmetry \(\Delta Y=1\) as well.</p><p>(b) \([X,Y]=2iZ\) and \(\langle 0|Z|0\rangle=1\), so the bound is \(\tfrac12|2i\cdot 1|=1\).</p><p>Since \(\Delta X\Delta Y=1\) equals the bound, \(|0\rangle\) is a minimum-uncertainty state for the pair \((X,Y)\) — the qubit analogue of a coherent state.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
