  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C2.S3 — Helstrom bound for two pure states</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C2.S3 — Optimal state discrimination</p></div><p>Two equally likely pure states satisfy \(\langle\psi_0|\psi_1\rangle=\cos\alpha\) with \(\alpha\in[0,\pi/2]\).</p><p>(a) Give the maximal probability of correctly identifying the prepared state, as a simplified function of <code>alpha</code>.</p><p>\(p_{\max}=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Evaluate it for \(\alpha=\pi/4\) (exact expression, no decimals).</p><p>\(p_{\max}(\pi/4)=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) The Helstrom bound for equal priors and pure states is \(p_{\max}=\tfrac12\big(1+\sqrt{1-|\langle\psi_0|\psi_1\rangle|^{2}}\big)\). With overlap \(\cos\alpha\) and \(\alpha\in[0,\pi/2]\), \(\sqrt{1-\cos^2\alpha}=\sin\alpha\), so \(p_{\max}=\tfrac12(1+\sin\alpha)\).</p><p>(b) At \(\alpha=\pi/4\): \(p_{\max}=\tfrac12\left(1+\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)=\tfrac{2+\sqrt2}{4}\approx0.8536\).</p><p>Only orthogonal states (\(\alpha=\pi/2\)) give \(p_{\max}=1\); identical states give \(1/2\), i.e. a coin flip. This is the quantitative form of the statement that non-orthogonal states cannot be reliably distinguished, and it underpins BB84.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
